<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ukrainians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ukrainians are an East Slavic people whose homeland is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Long divided between Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, they developed a national movement in the 19th century, strongest in Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. After short-lived independent states in 1917–1921, most lived in Soviet Ukraine until independence in 1991, with large minorities in interwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukrainians.jpg" alt="Ukrainians" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Ukrainian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a> emerged from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the migrations of the 6th and 7th centuries, and the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> united them from the 9th to the 13th century. East Slavic tribes called proto-Ukrainian include the Volhynians, Derevlianians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polans-eastern">Polianians</a> and Siverianians, and the Polianians are identified as the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. Varangians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> conquered the tribes in the 9th century and laid the groundwork for the state. The Mongol invasions of 1236 and 1240 destroyed it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia">Kingdom of Galicia–Volhynia</a> (1199–1349) followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> of Zaporizhzhia controlled the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> from the late 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> (1648–1782), and in the Ruin of the late 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> split into hostile Left-Bank and Right-Bank halves. Under the Russian Empire Ukrainians were called Little Russians and Ukrainian was outlawed in almost all contexts. Ivan Kotliarevsky's Eneyida of 1798 established the modern language, and a Romantic revival followed.</p><p>In 1918 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-rada">Central Rada</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykhailo-hrushevsky">Mykhailo Hrushevsky</a> declared the independence of the Ukrainian National Republic. In 1932 and 1933 millions starved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and many foreign governments and scholars have called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>; scholarly estimates of the direct loss of life range from 2.6 million to 12 million. Independent Ukraine arose in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The name Ukraintsi comes from Ukraina, first documented in the Kievan Chronicle under 1187. Ukrainian, written in a Cyrillic alphabet, descends from the Old East Slavic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, and most Ukrainians adhere to Eastern Orthodox Christianity. The name came into use in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> first and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> only late in the 19th century. Sub-groups in the west, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutsuls">Hutsuls</a>, Boykos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemkos">Lemkos</a>, keep their own dialects, dress and folk traditions. A diaspora lives in more than 120 countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ukrainians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Large-scale immigration under Wilfrid Laurier’s government after 1896, including <b>Ukrainians</b>, Germans, Scandinavians and Americans as well as Britons, settled the prairies, which became one of the world’s great wheat-growing regions, while Chinese labourers who had helped build the railway faced a head tax from 1885 and near-total exclusion…</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its population was mostly Poles, Polish Jews, ethnic Germans, <b>Ukrainians</b> and Lithuanians, with a small Russian minority, and mainly Roman Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its ethnic and religious make-up changed dramatically with expansion: by the fifteenth century Orthodox East Slavs, ancestors of today's Belarusians and <b>Ukrainians</b>, formed the large majority, while ethnic Lithuanians lived mainly in the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The population reached 51,706,746 in 1989, and by 1979 53% of ethnic <b>Ukrainians</b> lived in towns; Kiev grew from 1.1 million in 1959 to 2.1 million in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Romanians, <b>Ukrainians</b>, Jews, Germans and Poles lived side by side, and in 1910 the diet adopted a compromise sharing seats among national groups.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bukovina">Duchy of Bukovina</a></li><li><blockquote>It was a land of Poles, <b>Ukrainians</b> and a large Jewish population, known for rural poverty and mass emigration, but also for oil wells at Boryslav and a flourishing Ukrainian national movement.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria</a></li></ul></section>
